g to do their best too if you like  save the world but I am a bit of a new  boy a bit of a Johnny come lately in  comparison to Victoria Seabrook our  climate reporter Victoria you've been  here for more than 14 days you must be  really really in immersed in it by now  yes it does start to feel a bit like  Groundhog Day once you've been here for  a while but thankfully things are really  ramping up now as we as we get close to  the finish so the adrenaline Gets You  Through towards the end well what are  those things approaching the Finish Line  what are the things still under debate  yeah so we've had um movement on two key  things that that we really needed to see  if there was going to be a you know a  good outcome here really yeah and that's  happened in the last 24 hours so one of  the key issues is what we've heard so  much about here which is about who pays  for climate impacts climate damages in  vulnerable countries things like the  flooding in Pakistan because I heard  just 24 hours ago Antonio Gutierrez you  know boss of un came and said you know  there was a breakdown of trust between  the North and South of this and we  needed to Stand and Deliver great praise  um and there has been some movement  since then yes that's right he actually  jetted straight from the G20 he'd  already been here last week and decided  to come back to try and give the talks a  little bit of momentum what's happened  on that key issue is  that there's been a rift really up until  yesterday with on the one hand  developing countries saying we need a  fund to pay when climate disasters  strike and developed countries on the  other hand saying yes okay we  acknowledge that there needs to be some  some funding but we're not we we think  that actually it should be much broader  than just a fund there's lots of other  levers that we can pull they were really  at loggerheads up until about yesterday  and then yesterday evening potentially  there has there has been a big  breakthrough 