y promises and when the situation  in 2025 might be that they're not even  in power why do you think that they  haven't committed to doing it from now  I really don't know and that's probably  a question uh better posed to the  government because all of the experts  you know even fuel poverty groups  um the CCC everybody has been saying for  years that it's a good idea to sort out  our leaky homes and given the current  gas crisis you know there's public  demand for it as well people want to do  these things people want to fix up their  home so they can save energy and save  money on bills but they're not being  offered the kind of support that we're  seeing in many other European countries  and it really is you know a large  question mark over why that's the case  now that the base secretary the business  energy and Industrial uh strategy  secretary is due to make another  statement in the coming weeks  um around Energy Efficiency and around  energy demand reduction and but so forth  so far what we're seeing from the  government is a lot of talking and a lot  not a lot of walking and so it might be  that we see more in the coming weeks but  so far there's a large question mark  okay concrete step they did um hike the  windfall tax surely must be pleased with  that step  well the windfall tax you know lots of  the public are thinking that's a fair  thing to do given the large profits that  the oil and gas companies are making  during the gas crisis and but when it  comes to the renewable electricity  generation tax and the renewable sector  are a little bit baffled by this because  the oil and gas sector get taxpayer  money back for investing in new oil and  gas whereas the renewable electricity  generators don't get anything for  investing in new renewable electricity  and so there's a bit of a backward step  there when quite clearly the solution to  the gas crisis is moving away from gas  and moving to more Renewables and this  this loophole for the oil and gas  c